(176, 5, 'Level 18', 'GETTING INPUT', 'The Newline Trap', 'A trap lies on the path. The guide warns against scanning text with nextLine() right after nextInt() on the same scanner. nextInt() reads only the number and leaves the Enter key (the newline) behind, so the next nextLine() grabs that leftover and returns an empty String instead of the user\'s text. A common fix is to call scan.nextLine() once to throw away the leftover newline.

Read an int into age, discard the leftover newline with scan.nextLine(), then read a String into name.', 'Put an extra scan.nextLine(); between the two reads.', 'int age = scan.nextInt(); scan.nextLine(); String name = scan.nextLine();', 60, 8, 18, 1, '2026-10-09 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution: int age = scan.nextInt(); scan.nextLine(); String name = scan.nextLine();', 'int\\s+age\\s+=\\s+scan\\.nextInt\\(\\s*\\s*\\);\\s+scan\\.nextLine\\(\\s*\\s*\\);\\s+String\\s+name\\s+=\\s+scan\\.nextLine\\(\\s*\\s*\\);', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart2.png', 'nextInt() Then nextLine()', 'A trap lies on the path. The guide warns against scanning text with nextLine() right after nextInt() on the same scanner. nextInt() reads only the number and leaves the Enter key (the newline) behind, so the next nextLine() grabs that leftover and returns an empty String instead of the user\'s text. A common fix is to call scan.nextLine() once to throw away the leftover newline.

Read an int into age, discard the leftover newline with scan.nextLine(), then read a String into name.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'int age = scan.nextInt(); scan.nextLine(); String name = scan.nextLine();', '[{"part": "int age = scan.nextInt();", "explanation": "Reads the number but leaves the newline in the input."}, {"part": "scan.nextLine();", "explanation": "Throws away the leftover newline so it cannot be mistaken for the user\'s text."}, {"part": "String name = scan.nextLine();", "explanation": "Now reads the text the user really typed."}]'),
(177, 5, 'Level 19', 'GETTING INPUT', 'The File Gate', 'At the end of the isle Pip finds a scroll stored in a file. Reading a file takes three objects: a FileInputStream opens the file, a DataInputStream wraps it, and a BufferedReader reads it. In Java code a backslash in a path is typed twice, so the file C:\\hello.txt is written "C:\\\\hello.txt".

Open "C:\\\\hello.txt" with a FileInputStream named fstream, wrap it in a DataInputStream named in, then create a BufferedReader named br from in.', 'Three statements: fstream, then in, then br.', 'FileInputStream fstream = new FileInputStream("C:\\\\hello.txt"); DataInputStream in = new DataInputStream(fstream); BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(in));', 60, 8, 19, 1, '2026-10-09 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'FileInputStream\\s+fstream\\s+=\\s+new\\s+FileInputStream\\("C:\\\\\\\\hello\\.txt"\\);\\s+DataInputStream\\s+in\\s+=\\s+new\\s+DataInputStream\\(fstream\\);\\s+BufferedReader\\s+br\\s+=\\s+new\\s+BufferedReader\\(new\\s+InputStreamReader\\(in\\)\\);', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart3.png', 'Opening a Text File', 'At the end of the isle Pip finds a scroll stored in a file. Reading a file takes three objects: a FileInputStream opens the file, a DataInputStream wraps it, and a BufferedReader reads it. In Java code a backslash in a path is typed twice, so the file C:\\hello.txt is written "C:\\\\hello.txt".

Open "C:\\\\hello.txt" with a FileInputStream named fstream, wrap it in a DataInputStream named in, then create a BufferedReader named br from in.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'FileInputStream fstream = new FileInputStream("C:\\\\hello.txt"); DataInputStream in = new DataInputStream(fstream); BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(in));', '[{"part": "FileInputStream fstream = new FileInputStream(\\"C:\\\\\\\\hello.txt\\");", "explanation": "Opens the file at that path."}, {"part": "DataInputStream in = new DataInputStream(fstream);", "explanation": "Wraps the file stream in a DataInputStream object."}, {"part": "BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(in));", "explanation": "Builds a reader that can read the file line by line."}]'),
(178, 5, 'Level 20', 'GETTING INPUT', 'The Final Scroll', 'The scroll unrolls across the sky. A while loop reads the file one line at a time: br.readLine() returns the next line, and when there are no more lines it returns null, so the loop stops. Each line is stored in strLine and printed on the console. When the loop is done, close the stream with in.close().

Assume br and in already exist. Declare a String named strLine, loop while strLine = br.readLine() is not null, print strLine inside the loop, then close in.', 'The assignment sits in its own parentheses: ((strLine = br.readLine()) != null)', 'String strLine; while ((strLine = br.readLine()) != null) { System.out.println(strLine); } in.close();', 100, 8, 20, 1, '2026-10-09 08:00:00', 0, 0, 0, 'Use the exact solution shown in the goal.', 'String\\s+strLine;\\s+while\\s*\\(\\s*\\(\\s*strLine\\s+=\\s+br\\.readLine\\(\\s*\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*!=\\s+null\\s*\\)\\s*\\{\\s*System\\.out\\.println\\(\\s*strLine\\s*\\);\\s*\\}\\s*in\\.close\\(\\s*\\s*\\);', '/assets/landscapes/oakheart4.png', 'Reading a File Line by Line', 'The scroll unrolls across the sky. A while loop reads the file one line at a time: br.readLine() returns the next line, and when there are no more lines it returns null, so the loop stops. Each line is stored in strLine and printed on the console. When the loop is done, close the stream with in.close().

Assume br and in already exist. Declare a String named strLine, loop while strLine = br.readLine() is not null, print strLine inside the loop, then close in.', 10, 1, '{"tile_elevations": {}}', 10, 0.78000000, 1, 8, 'String strLine; while ((strLine = br.readLine()) != null) { System.out.println(strLine); } in.close();', '[{"part": "String strLine;", "explanation": "Holds each line that is read."}, {"part": "while (", "explanation": "Starts a loop that repeats while the condition is true."}, {"part": "(strLine = br.readLine())", "explanation": "Reads the next line and stores it in strLine. The extra parentheses make the assigned value available for the comparison."}, {"part": "!= null", "explanation": "readLine() returns null at the end of the file, which ends the loop."}, {"part": "{ System.out.println(strLine); }", "explanation": "Prints each line on the console."}, {"part": "in.close();", "explanation": "Closes the input stream when reading is finished."}]');

